Recommends: ocl-icd-libopencl1
Suggests: dsd-fme, python3, mesa-opencl-icd, nvidia-opencl-icd
Description: RC4 key recovery for DMR Enhanced Privacy voice
 dmrcrack brute-forces the 5-byte (40-bit) RC4 key used in DMR Enhanced
 Privacy encrypted voice transmissions. It takes demodulated voice frame
 payloads in .bin format (produced by DSD-FME with -Q DSP output) and
 exhaustively searches the 40-bit keyspace (up to ~1.1 trillion candidates).
 .
 The Debian package builds the portable OpenCL GPU back-end, which runs on
 any vendor's OpenCL driver (NVIDIA, AMD or Intel) and falls back to a
 multi-threaded CPU engine when no GPU is available. It has no proprietary
 build dependencies. Optional NVIDIA CUDA and AMD ROCm/HIP back-ends are
 available when building from source (-DUSE_CUDA=ON or -DUSE_HIP=ON).
 GPU/CPU work split, search range, and thread count are configurable, and
 progress is periodically checkpointed so an interrupted search can be
 resumed.
 .
 Typical workflow: capture an encrypted DMR session with a software-defined
 radio, demodulate it with DSD-FME using -Q DSP output mode, convert the
 output to .bin format with the included dsdfme_dsp_to_bin.py script, then
 run dmrcrack against the resulting payload file.
 .
 This is a security research and auditing tool. Use it only on
 transmissions you are authorised to analyse.
